{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and was the heir to the County of Tripoli he was raised in the court of his father and was trained in the arts of war and governance. \nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152 he was around 12 years old at that time and the regency was held by his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Reign* \nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a skilled military leader and fought against the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi. \nHe also allied himself with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to counter the growing threat of the Muslim forces. \n*Relationships and Alliances* \nRaymond III was married to Hodierna of Jerusalem's niece but the marriage was childless and he later married a woman named Salerna but the marriage was also childless. \nHe had a close relationship with King Amalric I of Jerusalem and the two leaders worked together to counter the threat of the Muslim forces. \nRaymond III also had a close relationship with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos and the two leaders worked together to counter the threat of the Muslim forces. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \nHe was remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \nRaymond III was also known for his relationships and alliances with other leaders of the time including the Byzantine Emperor and the King of Jerusalem. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III left a lasting legacy in the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusal